{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1779457040,"nonce":"L8pZIwXcrUvFC+Pn4jrMkBxMg04=","message":"## UX policy confirmed for conflict handling\n\nFor this child, use the following connect-time policy unless later implementation evidence forces a narrower behavior:\n\n- Block structural impossibilities before edge creation: wrong direction, unknown node/port, occupied single-input target, or graph shape that cannot be represented.\n- For known dimension conflicts that express plausible design intent, keep the attempted edge as invalid draft topology and make the conflict explicit. The edge should not imply the graph is executable; validation and the affected parameter row should show the problem clearly.\n- If the conflict has one unambiguous resolution and auto-sync is enabled, apply the parameter update instead of leaving an error.\n- If the user has manually overridden the managed param, preserve the manual value and report the conflict with a resume-sync action.\n\nThe feel we want is constraint-solving authoring: users can sketch a model connection and let Studio synchronize dimensions when safe, but impossible execution states remain visually and textually obvious until resolved.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Codex (GPT-5) \u003ccodex@openai.com\u003e","files":null}]}